Feature: Remote ID
  A plane that sends [[Remote ID]] tells every receiver near it who it is and where it is.
  The receiver is on your plane, not at the station. The range is the range from your plane
  to the other plane: about a kilometre at best. The station gets the broadcast down the
  radio link, a few seconds later.

  Expectation: A plane that sends Remote ID gets a marker
    Given the other plane is sending Remote ID
    And the distance to the other plane is at most 500 m
    When 5 seconds pass
    Then the map has 1 other plane
    And the marker of the other plane is live
    And the source of the marker is Remote ID

  Expectation: Remote ID does not reach far
    The receiver on your plane is small. The broadcast of a plane that is more than
    1.5 km away does not reach it.
    Given the other plane is sending Remote ID
    And the distance to the other plane is at least 1500 m
    And the map has 0 other planes
    When 5 seconds pass
    Then the map has 0 other planes

  Expectation: Your own transmitter is not another plane
    The Remote ID transmitter on your plane sends the ID of your plane to the autopilot. The
    station does not show your plane twice.
    Given your Remote ID is on
    And the other plane is silent
    When 5 seconds pass
    Then the map has 0 other planes
